evidence.quote:
For every $n\geq 1$ and $a\in K$, there is a unique point $R_n(a)\in K$ satisfying $$\label{eq:resolvent-definition}
 R_n(a)=\frac{a}{n}+\left(1-\frac1n\right)T R_n(a).$$ The map $R_n:K\to K$ is nonexpansive, and $$\label{eq:resolvent-estimates}
 \norm{T R_n(a)-R_n(a)}\leq\frac1n,
 \qquad
 \norm{R_n(a)-a}\leq(n-1)\norm{Ta-a}
 \quad(a\in K).$$

The set $A$ is a nonempty compact semigroup of nonexpansive selfmaps of $K$ and contains an idempotent $P$. There is a point $x\in K$ with $P(x)=x$ such that the nonempty weakly compact set $$\label{eq:anchor-tail-set}
 D_x=\bigcap_{m\geq1}
       \overline{\{h(x):h\in\mathcal H_m\}}^{\,\mathrm{weak}}$$ satisfies $$\label{eq:anchor-convex-hull}
 x\in\overline{\mathop{\mathrm{conv}}D_x}^{\,\norm{\cdot}}.$$

